(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-anus-rectum-quiz"]={"title":"Anus & Rectum Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"During the health history for the anorectal exam, hematochezia refers to...","choices":["absence of stool for several days","straining during defecation","black, tarry stool","visible bloody stool"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hematochezia is the passage of visibly bloody stool, one of the key symptoms specifically asked about in the anorectal health history, along with pain on defecation and perianal lesions. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"The standard positioning for the anorectal examination has the patient...","choices":["standing upright and bending forward sharply at the waist","lying supine with both legs extended fully straight","lying prone, face down, with both arms at the sides","lying on their left side with hips and knees partially flexed"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anorectal exam is classically performed with the patient in the left lateral (side-lying) position with hips and knees partially flexed, allowing good visualization and access to the perianal area. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"The physical exam of the anus and rectum proceeds through which sequence of components?","choices":["Only the DRE (digital rectal examination) is performed; visual inspection is not part of this exam","Perform the DRE (digital rectal examination) first, then inspect the anus and perianal areas afterward","Inspect the sacrococcygeal/perianal areas, inspect the anus, then perform a DRE (digital rectal examination)","Palpate the rectal wall first via DRE (digital rectal examination), then inspect the perianal areas and anus afterward"],"answer":2,"exp":"The exam proceeds from external inspection (sacrococcygeal and perianal areas, then the anus itself) before moving to the internal DRE (digital rectal examination). — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"During inspection of the anus and perianal region, the examiner should specifically look for...","choices":["bowel sound frequency and pitch, assessed during abdominal auscultation","lesions, ulcers, inflammation, rashes, excoriations, and external skin breakdown","liver span measured by careful abdominal percussion technique alone","blood pressure and pulse, measured routinely as part of the vital signs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inspection of the anus and rectum region specifically screens for lesions, ulcers, inflammation, rashes, excoriations, and other external skin breakdown, as well as masses or tenderness, before any palpation. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"DRE (digital rectal examination), after lubricating the gloved index finger and inserting the fingertip into the anal canal, the examiner should next ask the patient to...","choices":["hold their breath for 30 seconds","cough forcefully","stand up immediately","squeeze the anal sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"After the fingertip is inserted, the examiner asks the patient to squeeze the anal sphincter around the finger, which allows assessment of sphincter tone, before proceeding to palpate the rectal surface more broadly. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Among the examples of anorectal abnormalities the exam may detect, which grouping is correct?","choices":["Cardiac murmurs, diastolic gallops, and pericardial friction rubs heard on auscultation","Abdominal borborygmi and vascular bruits heard on routine abdominal auscultation exams","Hemorrhoids, anal fissures or warts, sphincter tightness or laxity, perianal abscesses, and malignancies","Pulmonary wheezes, rhonchi, and inspiratory crackles heard on routine lung auscultation"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific examples of anorectal abnormalities listed: hemorrhoids, anal fissures/warts, abnormal sphincter tone (tightness or laxity), perianal abscesses, and malignancies. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Special techniques and maneuvers associated with the anorectal exam include performing a prostatic examination and a rectovaginal examination, the latter of which is performed in which position?","choices":["Prone position","Lithotomy position","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral (side-lying) position"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rectovaginal examination, one of the special maneuvers associated with the anorectal exam, is performed with the patient in the lithotomy position. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"During the DRE (digital rectal examination), after the patient squeezes the anal sphincter, the examiner proceeds to palpate the rectal surface, systematically assessing for...","choices":["heart murmurs, diastolic gallops, and rubs on cardiac auscultation","thyroid nodules and overall gland size on neck palpation","lung crackles and wheezes heard on pulmonary auscultation","masses, tenderness, and irregularities of the rectal mucosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Systematic palpation of the rectal surface during the DRE (digital rectal examination) screens for masses, tenderness, and mucosal irregularities that might indicate polyps, tumors, or other pathology. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"During the prostatic examination (performed via the rectal approach), the examiner assesses the prostate for...","choices":["size, symmetry, consistency, and the presence of nodules or tenderness","bowel sound frequency, assessed by abdominal auscultation instead","blood pressure and heart rate only, as routine vital signs","skin turgor, assessed elsewhere as a marker of hydration status"],"answer":0,"exp":"The digital prostate exam evaluates the gland's size, symmetry (comparing the two lateral lobes), consistency (should be smooth and rubbery, not stony-hard), and checks for any nodules or areas of tenderness. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"An anal fissure classically presents with which typical location and symptom pattern?","choices":["A firm, non-tender nodule palpable only on DRE (digital rectal examination)","A tear most often in the posterior midline, causing sharp pain with defecation","A painless mass located laterally, entirely unrelated to defecation timing","Diffuse perianal itching present continuously with no visible lesion at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Anal fissures classically occur in the posterior midline (due to relatively reduced blood supply there) and cause sharp, tearing pain during and after defecation, often with a small amount of bright red bleeding. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slides 7, 11"},{"q":"Internal hemorrhoids differ from external hemorrhoids primarily in that internal hemorrhoids...","choices":["arise above the dentate (pectinate) line and are generally not palpable/visible unless prolapsed","require no special positioning or additional instrumentation whatsoever to detect","are always significantly more painful than external hemorrhoids upon direct palpation","are located exclusively on the visible external perianal skin surface, never internally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Internal hemorrhoids originate above the dentate line, in an area with visceral innervation, so they are typically painless and not visible/palpable unless they prolapse; external hemorrhoids arise below the dentate line, in somatically innervated skin, and are often visible and can be quite painful. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"A key clinical use of the DRE (digital rectal examination), beyond palpating the rectal wall and prostate, is to...","choices":["directly visualize the entire sigmoid colon through the anal canal","obtain a stool sample from the examining glove to test for occult blood","assess symmetric chest wall expansion during quiet respiration","measure blood pressure directly from within the rectal wall itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"A standard part of the DRE (digital rectal examination) is checking any stool present on the examining glove for occult (hidden) blood, which can be an important screening step for GI (gastrointestinal) bleeding even when hematochezia is not grossly visible. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Besides the standard left-lateral position, an alternative position sometimes used for the anorectal exam (particularly convenient when combined with a pelvic exam) is the...","choices":["lithotomy position","Trendelenburg position","standing on one leg","reverse Trendelenburg position"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lithotomy position, already used for pelvic examination, can also conveniently accommodate an anorectal exam in the same setup, complementing the standard left-lateral position used more generally. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Beyond hematochezia, other key elements of the anorectal health history include pain on defecation and...","choices":["visual field disturbances","anal and perianal lesions","chest pain on exertion","hearing loss or tinnitus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anorectal health history specifically screens for hematochezia, pain on defecation, and anal/perianal lesions, giving a focused symptom set that guides the subsequent physical exam. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A perianal abscess, one of the listed anorectal abnormalities, typically presents on exam as...","choices":["complete absence of any visible or palpable finding","a tender, fluctuant, erythematous swelling near the anus","a painless, firm, non-mobile mass","a symmetric bluish discoloration of the perianal skin with no tenderness"],"answer":1,"exp":"A perianal abscess classically presents as a tender, fluctuant (compressible, suggesting fluid collection), erythematous (red/inflamed) swelling adjacent to the anus, reflecting a localized collection of infected fluid. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"A prostate that feels stony-hard with an irregular nodule on DRE (digital rectal examination) is most concerning for...","choices":["normal aging changes only","a normal finding requiring no further workup","a full bladder","malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"A stony-hard, irregular, nodular prostate on DRE (digital rectal examination) is the classic finding raising concern for prostate malignancy, in contrast to the smooth, symmetric, rubbery consistency of a normal prostate. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Wearing gloves throughout the anorectal exam serves primarily to...","choices":["warm the examiner's hands prior to any patient contact at all","make additional lubrication entirely unnecessary throughout the exam","protect both patient and examiner from contamination/infection","substantially improve the examiner's fine tactile sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Standard precautions require gloves for any exam involving contact with mucous membranes or body fluids, protecting both the patient and the examiner from potential infection transmission. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Anal warts (condyloma acuminata) typically appear on inspection as...","choices":["small, flesh-colored, cauliflower-like papules around the anus","a single deep linear tear located in the posterior midline","a smooth, fluctuant, tender swelling located near the anus","complete and diffuse loss of perianal skin pigmentation entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Anal warts (condyloma acuminata), caused by HPV (human papillomavirus), classically appear as small, flesh-colored, cauliflower-like (verrucous) papules or clusters around the anal region. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Adequate draping during the anorectal exam serves to...","choices":["completely and permanently prevent the ongoing need for a chaperone present during the exam","completely and permanently eliminate the separate need for adequate examination room lighting","preserve patient dignity/comfort while still allowing adequate exposure of the area being examined","completely and permanently replace the separate need for documented verbal patient consent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Proper draping balances patient dignity and comfort with the examiner's need for adequate visualization and access to the perianal area, exposing only what is necessary for the exam. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A patient reporting pain specifically during defecation, as part of the anorectal history, should prompt the examiner to particularly consider conditions such as...","choices":["asymptomatic internal hemorrhoids only","hearing changes","a normal, unremarkable history requiring no further exam","anal fissure or thrombosed hemorrhoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pain specifically with defecation is a hallmark symptom of conditions like an anal fissure or a thrombosed external hemorrhoid, both of which are painful, defecation-related anorectal conditions. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Palpating any abnormal areas noted on inspection (per the exam sequence) should occur...","choices":["only if the patient specifically requests that step","before any visual inspection has even begun to occur","after visual inspection has identified the area of concern","only during the separate digital prostate portion of the exam"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sequence is inspect first, then palpate any abnormal areas identified — palpation is targeted based on what inspection reveals, not performed blindly before looking. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"A history of chronic constipation and straining is relevant to the anorectal exam because it is a recognized risk factor for...","choices":["hearing loss","hemorrhoids and anal fissures","prostate cancer exclusively","no anorectal conditions at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chronic straining (as with constipation) increases pressure in the anal canal, a well-recognized risk factor for developing both hemorrhoids and anal fissures. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A rectovaginal exam, one of the special maneuvers listed, is particularly useful for assessing...","choices":["the rectovaginal septum and posterior pelvic structures not reachable by a rectal exam alone","lung sounds, assessed separately through routine pulmonary auscultation technique","visual acuity, assessed separately through a formal ophthalmologic screening exam","thyroid size, assessed separately through careful anterior neck palpation technique"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectovaginal exam (one finger in the rectum, one in the vagina) allows assessment of the rectovaginal septum and posterior pelvic structures (like the uterosacral ligaments or posterior uterus/adnexa) that a rectal exam alone cannot fully evaluate. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"A thrombosed external hemorrhoid classically presents as...","choices":["a normal finding in most adults","diffuse perianal erythema with no discrete lump","a sudden, painful, bluish, tender perianal lump","a painless, slowly enlarging mass"],"answer":2,"exp":"A thrombosed external hemorrhoid presents as an acutely painful, tender, bluish (from clotted blood) lump at the anal verge, often developing suddenly. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Watching the patient's face during the anorectal exam is a recommended practice mainly to...","choices":["gauge discomfort/pain in real time and adjust technique accordingly","formally assess the patient's visual acuity and eye movements","check specifically for signs of jaundice in the sclera","completely replace the ongoing need to ask about pain verbally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Observing facial expressions provides real-time feedback about patient discomfort or pain, allowing the examiner to adjust pressure/technique or pause as needed for patient comfort. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Bright red blood noted only on the toilet paper (not mixed in the stool) after a bowel movement most classically points toward...","choices":["a pulmonary source, unrelated to the digestive tract","an upper GI (gastrointestinal) bleed such as a peptic ulcer","a normal finding requiring no further clinical evaluation","a distal source such as hemorrhoids or an anal fissure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Blood that appears only on wiping (toilet paper), rather than mixed throughout the stool, most classically points to a distal (anal/perianal) source such as hemorrhoids or a fissure, rather than a more proximal GI (gastrointestinal) bleed. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A normal, healthy prostate on DRE (digital rectal examination) should feel...","choices":["stony-hard in consistency, with multiple palpable irregular nodules","smooth, symmetric, and rubbery/firm (like the tip of the nose), non-tender","boggy, soft in texture, and exquisitely tender throughout to palpation","not palpable at all through the anterior rectal wall on exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"A normal prostate should feel smooth-surfaced, symmetric between its two lobes, and have a firm-but-rubbery consistency (often likened to the tip of the nose), without tenderness or nodularity. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Documenting current medications (for example, anticoagulants) is relevant before the anorectal exam mainly because...","choices":["medications never meaningfully affect any physical exam findings whatsoever at all","documenting it completely and permanently replaces the ongoing need for the physical exam","anticoagulant use increases bleeding risk and can affect interpretation of minor bleeding on exam","it is only relevant to the entirely separate cardiac history and physical exam findings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Patients on anticoagulants (or antiplatelet agents) have an increased bleeding tendency, which is important both for safety during the exam and for correctly interpreting any minor bleeding noted, since it may be exaggerated by the medication rather than reflecting more significant pathology. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Evaluating for rectal prolapse, a special finding sometimes assessed during this exam, is best done by...","choices":["measuring the patient's seated blood pressure carefully before the maneuver begins","asking the patient to bear down/strain and observing for protrusion of rectal tissue","auscultating the abdomen carefully across all four quadrants beforehand","carefully palpating the thyroid gland for size, symmetry, and nodularity beforehand"],"answer":1,"exp":"Rectal prolapse (full-thickness protrusion of the rectum through the anus) is best elicited by asking the patient to bear down or strain, which increases intra-abdominal pressure and can reveal the prolapse if present. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Ensuring the patient is comfortable and adequately draped before beginning the anorectal exam should occur...","choices":["it is not a relevant consideration for this exam","only if the patient specifically requests it","only after the DRE (digital rectal examination) is already complete","before the exam begins, as part of preparing the patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Patient comfort and appropriate draping should be established before starting the exam, as part of the preparatory steps alongside positioning and explaining the exam. — Anus and Rectum Examination lecture, Slide 6"}]};
